msgid "` A Bluetooth Data Link , such as an `HC-06 module <https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hc-06>`__ can be used to connect your Pixhawk (or other controller) to your Bluetooth enabled PC or Android Ground station at distances up to 50m."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:15
#: a2873089f1d04b44a73e08ad4279a5e8
msgid "this module's baud rate usually must be changed from its default of 9600 to 57600 for use with ArduPilot. This is usually accomplished connecting it to a FTDI USB-serial adapter and using AT commands in a terminal program like Putty. See the last section of this page for more information on setting up the HC-06."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:18
#: 8a071967732546348cf75a1c88336e11
msgid "Connecting to the Autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:20
#: 19091ac62c274bc79b49bfaa6ca894b9
msgid "The Bluetooth data link often comes with both a DF13 6 pin and 6-to-5 pin connector which make it easy to connect to the autopilot's serial ports. Note that any unused serial port on the autopilot can be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:27
#: c66f8b3db0414a04b7ef52731456c85e
msgid "Once you have connected the Bluetooth data link you can power up the autopilot.  It is OK to use USB connected power while using the Bluetooth module connected to the autopilot as long the the total power of ALL autopilot connected peripherals do not exceed the computer's capability (usually 1A)"
msgstr ""

