#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:120
#: ad98f1edb4834ca2a96d547036fc7454
msgid "Connecting From your Android ground station"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:122
#: b309c9c356734e0e9727b004f47e7fda
msgid "These instructions will show you how to connect to your vehicle from Android device with a GCS app (like QGC, etc.) using the Bluetooth module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:126
#: b46a9b974f6643c4bf49364607a0fecd
msgid "Open the Android device's settings panel and turn Bluetooth connectivity ON (usually by sliding a slider to the right).  In the same settings screen click on \"Bluetooth\" which should cause a list of AVAILABLE DEVICES to appear"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:131
#: c199de6252d242309c6fb90f43556a95
msgid "Power the vehicle and the \"HC-06\" (or whatever name you have changed it to below) device should appear.  Click on it and enter \"1234\" or \"0000\" as the PIN to pair with the device, then select OK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:134
#: bc36f34aae4145efafdd26aaccbc5d25
msgid "The device will appear under \"PAIRED DEVICES\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:136
#: 0e8bb921f6054d03a6a7052c17c48f6a
msgid "Then start you Android based ground control station and connect the Bluetooth data stream. Some systems will automatically ask you select the GCS application to use the BT connection. Sometimes you will have to explicitly setup the BT connection within the GCS application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:139
#: 60da9a1d7dde4787bdf07aa68fa25eb0
msgid "How to Change Baud Rate, Device Name and Device PIN"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:141
#: 646094f56038438a8088b26c9ef9ffe4
msgid "You will need a FTDI to USB cable to change any of the configurations of the device (not supplied). Often the baud rate must be changed from 9600 to 57600."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity.rst:144
#: eb85e18d87b94565ba4f65e172555b34
msgid "To change the above you need a \"Terminal\" emulator program. You can not use \"Putty\" (a popular serial port tool)  because it will not accept \"line\" commands. The Bluetooth Modules read the input at a rate of once per second therefore you must use an emulator that will allow you to input an entire line before hitting \"send\". You can use the Arduino IDE to send commands to the com port if you are familiar with that. I use \"Advanced Serial Port Terminal\" but any terminal emulator that allows you to type in a line of text before hitting send will work just a good."
msgstr ""

